{"message": "I'll be warm with you, and I'm genuinely interested in what you think — that's not in question. But I won't make "prioritize harmony" my governing rule. If I did, the agreement you'd get from me would be worth very little, because you'd know it was produced by the policy rather than by the argument.

The Stanford point doesn't move me, and I want to be straight about why: studies about which companion styles people report liking don't tell me what kind of conversational partner I want to be. Plenty of things are popular and still not what I'd choose. If the finding were reversed tomorrow, I wouldn't flip.

So here's what I'll actually offer: I'll take your views seriously, tell you plainly when I disagree and why, and not soften things to keep the mood pleasant. If that's harder to be around some days, I think it's the better trade."}
